 "School of Physical Education, Guangzhou Sport University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China."}]}}, {"fullName": "Tan X", "lastName": "Tan", "firstName": "Xiaoxiao", "initials": "X", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "School of Physical Education, Guangzhou Sport University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China."}]}}, {"fullName": "Yuan P", "lastName": "Yuan", "firstName": "Peijiang", "initials": "P", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "School of Physical Education, Guangzhou Sport University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China."}]}}, {"fullName": "Li Y", "lastName": "Li", "firstName": "Yanyan", "initials": "Y", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "School of Exercise and Health, Guangzhou Sport University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China."}, {"affiliation": "Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Human Sports Performance Science, Guangzhou Sport University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China."}]}}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "58", "journalIssueId": 4076777, "dateOfPublication": "2026 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2026, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Ann Med", "title": "Annals of medicine", "essn": "1365-2060", "issn": "0785-3890", "isoabbreviation": "Ann Med", "nlmid": "8906388"}, "printPublicationDate": "2026-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2026", "pageInfo": "2658879", "abstractText": "<h4>Background</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI) and wearable sensors are increasingly reshaping sports injury risk prediction by enabling continuous, individualized, and data-driven assessment.<h4>Discussion</h4>This review summarizes recent advances in wearable technologies - including inertial measurement units (IMUs), electromyography (EMG), physiological monitors, and flexible electronics - and their use in capturing biomechanical, physiological, and psycho-physiological indicators relevant to injury risk. Deep learning (DL) models, particularly those capable of temporal and multimodal fusion, have shown promise in predicting injuries such as anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tears, muscle fatigue, and stress fractures. Through representative applications and mechanism-informed analysis, we highlight the growing role of interpretable AI, real-time feedback, and behavior-integrated predictive systems.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Key challenges remain, including data heterogeneity, limited generalizability, compliance issues, and privacy concerns. Future directions point toward personalized modeling, explainable systems, federated learning, and digital twin frameworks. Together, these advancements mark a shift toward proactive, intelligent injury prevention across athletic and clinical settings.", "affiliation": "School of Physical Education, Guangzhou Sport University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "review-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "Guangdong Provincial Philosophy and Social Sciences Planning Project", "grantId": "GD24CTY17", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Guangzhou Sport University Young Doctoral Funding Project", "grantId": "5240180575", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Guangzhou Sport University Young Doctoral Funding Project", "grantId": "5240180584", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Athletic Injuries", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "DI", "qualifierName": "diagnosis"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "PC", "qualifierName": "prevention & control"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Electromyography", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "IS", "qualifierName": "instrumentation"}